/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   firestore.rules — CUSTOMER ACCOUNTS addendum
   © 2025–2026 ClearSky Energy Solutions LLC. Proprietary and Confidential.

   Paste inside `service cloud.firestore { match /databases/…/documents {`,
   deploy, then grep the LIVE rules for `customers` to confirm it landed.
   Nothing here is live until that deploy happens — same as the control-plane
   and plant addenda.

   Design: docs/CUSTOMER-PORTAL.md §2. Three levels, not two:

       omega_orgs/{orgId}                          the white-label tenant
       omega_orgs/{orgId}/customers/{customerId}   a CUSTOMER ACCOUNT
       …/customers/{customerId}/users/{emailLower} the people in it

   ── WHY NEITHER IS READABLE BY THE PERSON IT DESCRIBES ───────────────────
   A customer account carries `terms` — net days, discount, credit limit. A
   customer who could read their own record could read their own discount,
   and a customer who could WRITE it would set their own price. Firestore
   rules hide documents, not fields, so there is no read rule that returns
   the account without the commercial terms inside it.

   So the portal never touches Firestore. api/my-account.js and
   api/my-orders.js project what a customer may see, key by key, exactly as
   api/embed-config.js does for the public storefront. These rules therefore
   grant the TENANT and ClearSky, and nobody else — which looks restrictive
   and is the only arrangement that keeps terms private while still letting a
   customer see their own orders.

   ── WHY THE TENANT MAY WRITE, BUT NOT MOVE ───────────────────────────────
   A tenant admin edits terms, plan and status freely: their customer, their
   commercial relationship. What they may not do is change `orgId`, because a
   customer account that can move between tenants is a customer account that
   can be taken to a competitor. Same trap `deals` guards with
   touchesAttribution() and `projects` guards on orgsInvolved.

   ── DELETE IS NOT GRANTED ────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Merging duplicate accounts moves ORDERS, and CLAUDE.md is explicit that a
   migration script flags rather than drops. A merged account is marked
   `mergedInto` and kept; nothing in the product deletes a customer record,
   so no rule permits it.
   ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

/* ── customer accounts ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
match /omega_orgs/{orgId}/customers/{customerId} {

  /* The tenant that sells to them, and us. NOT the customer: see the header. */
  allow read: if isOmegaStaff() || isTenantAdmin(orgAlias(orgId));

  /* Created by api/my-account.js on a customer's first sign-in (Admin SDK,
     which these rules do not constrain) or by a tenant admin setting up a
     sales-led account. A browser-created record is pinned to this org. */
  allow create: if (isOmegaStaff() || isTenantAdmin(orgAlias(orgId)))
                && request.resource.data.get('orgId', orgId) == orgId
                && request.resource.data.get('status', 'active') in ['active', 'suspended'];

  /* Terms, plan and status are the tenant's to set. orgId and createdAt are
     not theirs to change, and `mergedInto` is written by a script, never by a
     browser — a merge that a page could trigger is a merge nobody reviewed. */
  allow update: if (isOmegaStaff() || isTenantAdmin(orgAlias(orgId)))
                && request.resource.data.get('orgId', orgId) == resource.data.get('orgId', orgId)
                && request.resource.data.get('createdAt', '') == resource.data.get('createdAt', '')
                && request.resource.data.get('mergedInto', '') == resource.data.get('mergedInto', '');

  allow delete: if false;          /* flag, don't drop */
}

/* ── the people on an account ──────────────────────────────────────────── */
match /omega_orgs/{orgId}/customers/{customerId}/users/{emailLower} {

  allow read: if isOmegaStaff() || isTenantAdmin(orgAlias(orgId));

  /* A tenant admin may invite and remove people from their customer's
     account. The person's own profile edits go through api/my-account.js,
     which is also what stamps `uid` — a uid the client chose is a uid the
     client chose, and it is the only field here that could be used to
     impersonate. */
  allow create, update: if (isOmegaStaff() || isTenantAdmin(orgAlias(orgId)))
                        && request.resource.data.get('role', 'user') in ['owner', 'user'];

  /* Removing somebody from an account is not destroying a record of a person:
     the orders they placed stay on the account they placed them against. */
  allow delete: if isOmegaStaff() || isTenantAdmin(orgAlias(orgId));
}

/* ── the pointer collection ────────────────────────────────────────────── */
match /omega_orgs/{orgId}/customer_index/{emailLower} {
  /* email -> customerId, written by api/my-account.js with create() so two
     concurrent first sign-ins cannot both make an account. It exists because
     a collectionGroup('users') lookup needed a COLLECTION_GROUP index that
     Firestore does not auto-create, and the failed query was being swallowed
     into "no account found" — which made a duplicate account on every
     request rather than failing loudly.

     Admin SDK only. It is an index, not a record: nothing reads it but the
     endpoint, and a client that could write it could point somebody else's
     email at an account of its choosing. */
  allow read:  if isOmegaStaff() || isTenantAdmin(orgAlias(orgId));
  allow write: if false;
}

/* ── indexes to create alongside this ──────────────────────────────────────
   customers            : plan ASC, createdAt DESC            (designer seats)
   customers            : hasOrders ASC, createdAt DESC       (the admin list)
   orders               : orgId ASC, customerId ASC, createdAt DESC
   orders               : orgId ASC, customer.email ASC, createdAt DESC
                          ^ the claim path — docs/CUSTOMER-PORTAL.md §3
   NOT NEEDED: a collection-group index on users.email. The pointer document
   above replaced that query — a direct get() needs no index, does not scan
   other tenants, and cannot be swallowed into a duplicate-account create.
   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
